Q: Virtual range initializer in C++ to be used in range-based for loop A: <p>As others have pointed out, the <code>int end() { return -1; }</code> can be tricky as there is no native comparison between pointers and integers.<br />
Also compilers which don't support C++17 treat different return types on <code>begin()</code> and <code>end()</code> as error.</p>
<p>The probably easiest solution would be a <code>TestRange* end() { return nullptr; }</code>.<br />
One big problem of your shown code is that it would produce memory leaks during every iteration.<br />
You should use smart pointers or return raw pointers or references to instances, that are already stored and kept alive somewhere else, e.g. in a container on the current instance.<br />
Besides that I see no problems with your approach except that it is a bit unusual.</p>
<p><strong>Bonus hint:</strong><br />
With the raw pointers you could even use a covariant signature on your subtypes. I won't be surprised if this feature is unknown as there are only very rare situations where this can be used.<br />
In short: If you return a raw pointer or reference, then even the function signature on the subtypes is allowed to return a narrower type instead of the type defined on the derived signature (<code>TestRangeB*</code> instead of <code>TestRange*</code> and <code>TestRangeInitializerB*</code> instead of <code>TestRangeInitializer*</code>).<br />
Note that this works only with raw pointers and references, but not with any templates like smart pointers. This is the reason why it can be used only in rare situations.</p>
<p>Instead of</p>
<pre><code>class B : public A {
    class TestRangeB : public TestRange;
    class TestRangeInitializerB : public TestRangeInitializer {
        TestRange* begin() override {
            return new TestRangeB();
        }
    };
    TestRangeInitializer* getTestRangeInitializer() {
        return new TestRangeInitializerB();
    };
};
</code></pre>
<p>you can declare it as</p>
<pre><code>class B : public A {
    class TestRangeB : public TestRange;
    class TestRangeInitializerB : public TestRangeInitializer {
        TestRangeB* begin() override {
            return new TestRangeB();
        }
    };
    TestRangeInitializerB* getTestRangeInitializer() {
        return new TestRangeInitializerB();
    };
};
</code></pre>
<p>Thus anybody who is aware that the <code>TestRangeInitializer</code> and iterator were generated from a <code>B</code> can see and access <code>B</code>-specific details on the returned instances.</p>

Q: NgRX store select returns store state instead of data A: <p>The name provided in the reducer import part <em>must</em> be the same as the parameter name of the <code>State</code>.</p>
<p>Store:</p>
<pre><code>export interface AppState {
  logs: string[];
}

export interface AppStore{
  appState: AppState; // (1)
}
</code></pre>
<p><em>app.module.ts</em> import:</p>
<pre><code>StoreModule.forRoot({
  appState: reducerFunction // (2)
})
</code></pre>
<p>So, (1) and (2) (<code>appState</code>) <em>must</em> be the same.</p>

Q: Can you access a React component&#39;s children of children (nested children)? A: <p>You are misunderstanding what the React documentation is saying.</p>
<p>It says:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>There is no way to get <em><strong>the rendered output of an inner component</strong></em> like <code>&lt;MoreRows /&gt;</code> when manipulating children.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The re-emphasis is mine. Rendered output isn't the same thing as accessing component props. You can't access the rendered output, but you can certainly still access a nested component's <code>children</code> prop, i.e. <code>nestedComponent.props.children</code>.</p>
<p>The problem you face however is that the <code>MoreRows</code> component hasn't any children to access, i.e. <code>child.props.children</code> is <em>nothing</em>, so there's nothing to map and wrap further into a <code>div</code> element.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function MoreRows() { // &lt;-- no children prop
  return ( // &lt;-- no children rendered
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;This is the second item.&lt;/p&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;This is the third item.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}
</code></pre>
<p>What <code>MoreRows</code> renders directly is considered the &quot;children&quot; of the parent component rendering <em>it</em>, <code>RowList</code> in this case.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Is it possible to access the children of <code>&lt;MoreRows /&gt;</code> and to display each child in their own <code>&lt;div className=&quot;row&quot;&gt;...&lt;/div&gt;</code>?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes, sort of, if <code>MoreRows</code> actually consumes and renders a <code>children</code> prop it can, otherwise, no, it doesn't consider its direct content to be its &quot;children&quot; and can't split up what is in the React fragment.</p>
<p>Consider the following minor refactor where <code>MoreRows</code> is updated to consume a <code>children</code> prop and those two additional rows are passed <em><strong>as children</strong></em>.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="true" data-babel-preset-react="true" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function RowList({ children }) {
  return (
    &lt;div className="RowList"&gt;
      {React.Children.map(children, (child, index) =&gt; {
        if (Array.isArray(child.props.children)) {
          return React.Children.map(child.props.children, nestedChild =&gt;
            &lt;div className="Row nested"&gt;
              {nestedChild}
            &lt;/div&gt;
          )
        } else {
          return (
            &lt;div className="Row"&gt;
              {child}
            &lt;/div&gt;
          )
        }
      })}
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}

function MoreRows({ children }) {
  return (
    &lt;&gt;
      {children}
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}

function App() {
  return (
    &lt;RowList&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;This is the first item.&lt;/p&gt;
      &lt;MoreRows&gt;
        &lt;p&gt;This is the second item.&lt;/p&gt;
        &lt;p&gt;This is the third item.&lt;/p&gt;
      &lt;/MoreRows&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;This is the fourth item.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/RowList&gt;
  );
}

ReactDOM.render(
  &lt;App /&gt;,
  document.getElementById('root')
);</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.RowList {
  border: 2px solid grey;
  padding: 20px;
}

.Row {
  border: 2px dashed black;
  padding: 15px;
  margin: 10px;
}

.nested {
  border-color: blue;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/react/18.3.1/umd/react.production.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/react-dom/18.3.1/umd/react-dom.production.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;div id="root" /&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
